<exercises xml:id="exercises-subspaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <!-- Exercise group -->
    <xi:include href="./exercises.d/subspace-test.ptx"/>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-subspaces-analyze-span">
        <title>Analyzing a Span of vectors</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider <m>\Span S</m> for <m>S = \{(1,2,4),(-1,2,4)\}</m>.
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Determine if <m>\uvec{v}=(5,-2,4)</m> is in
                            <m>\Span S</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>

                    <answer>
                        <p>
                            Let's try to express <m>\uvec{v}</m> as a linear
                            combination of the vectors in <m> S</m>.
                            Set
                            <md>
                                (5,-2,4)=s(1,2,4)+t(-1,2,4)
                            </md>
                            for unknown scalars <m>s,t</m>.
                            Combining the linear combination and comparing
                            components, we arrive at the following linear system
                            of equations:
                            <md>
                                \begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
			5 \amp = \amp s \amp - \amp t \text{,} \\
			-2 \amp = \amp 2s \amp + \amp 2t \text{,} \\
			4 \amp = \amp 4s \amp + \amp 4t \text{.}
		\end{sysofeqns}
                            </md>
                            We seek to determine if this system is consistent.
                            We can convert the system to an augmented matrix and
                            reduce:
                            <md>
                                \begin{abmatrix}{rr|r}
			 1 \amp  -1 \amp    5\\
			2\amp  2\amp   -2 \\
			 4 \amp  4 \amp  4 \\
		\end{abmatrix}\qquad\rowredarrow\qquad
	\begin{abmatrix}{rr|r}
			 1 \amp  -1\amp    5\\
			0\amp  4\amp  -12 \\
			0 \amp  0 \amp  8 \\
		\end{abmatrix}
                            </md>.
                            Since the last row implies that <m>0=8</m>, the
                            system is not consistent, so <m>(5,-2,4)</m> is not
                            in <m>\Span S</m>.
                        </p>
                    </answer>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Determine if <m>\uvec{w}=(2,0,0)</m> is in
                            <m>\Span S</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>

                    <answer>
                        <p>
                            Again, we set up a linear combination of the vectors
                            in <m>S</m>, and set it equal to
                            <m>\uvec{w}=(2,0,0)</m>:
                            <md>
                                (2,0,0)=s(1,2,4)+t(-1,2,4)
                            </md>
                            for unknown scalars <m>s,t</m>.
                            The linear system is now
                            <md>
                                \begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
			2 \amp = \amp s \amp - \amp t \text{,} \\
			0 \amp = \amp 2s \amp + \amp 2t \text{,} \\
			0 \amp = \amp 4s \amp + \amp 4t \text{.}
		\end{sysofeqns}
                            </md>.
                            We can convert the system to an augmented matrix and
                            reduce:
                            <md>
                                \begin{abmatrix}{rr|r}
			 1 \amp  -1 \amp    2\\
			2\amp  2\amp  0\\
			 4 \amp  4 \amp  0 \\
		\end{abmatrix}\qquad\rowredarrow\qquad
	\begin{abmatrix}{rr|r}
			 1 \amp  0\amp    1\\
			0\amp  1\amp  -1\\
			0 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
		\end{abmatrix}
                            </md>.
                            The system is consistent, and the reduced matrix
                            gives us that <m>s=1,t=-1</m>.
                            The vector <m>\uvec{w}=(2,0,0)</m> is in
                            <m>\Span S</m>.
                        </p>
                    </answer>
                </task>

                <task xml:id="exercise-subspaces-analyze-span-general">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Give a condition on the values of <m>a,b,c</m> so
                            that <m>\uvec{u}=(a,b,c)</m> is in <m>\Span S</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>

                    <answer>
                        <p>
                            We set this up the same way as in the previous
                            parts: We try to express <m>(a,b,c)</m> as as linear
                            combination of the vectors in <m>S</m>:
                            <md>
                                (a,b,c)=s(1,2,4)+t(-1,2,4)
                            </md>
                            Now the system is
                            <md>
                                \begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
			a \amp = \amp s \amp - \amp t \text{,} \\
			b \amp = \amp 2s \amp + \amp 2t \text{,} \\
			c \amp = \amp 4s \amp + \amp 4t \text{.}
		\end{sysofeqns}
                            </md>,
                            and when we build the augmented matrix, we have
                            unknown variables in the right hand column:
                            <md>
                                \begin{abmatrix}{rr|r}
			 1 \amp  -1 \amp    a\\
			2\amp  2\amp  b\\
			 4 \amp  4 \amp  c\\
		\end{abmatrix}
                            </md>
                            As we perform row operations, this will manipulate
                            the unknown <m>a,b,c</m>.
                            You can verify that the augmented matrix will reduce
                            to the following row echelon form:
                            <md>
                                \begin{abmatrix}{rr|c}
			 1 \amp  -1 \amp    a\\
			0\amp  4\amp  (b-2a)\\
			 0 \amp 0 \amp  (c-2b)\\
		\end{abmatrix}
                            </md>
                            Because the matrix is in row echelon form, the
                            system will be consistent as long as the last row is
                            all zeros.
                            This will occur when <m>c-2b=0</m>.
                            This means that <m>\uvec{u}=(a,b,c)</m> is in
                            <m>\Span S</m> exactly when <m>c-2b=0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </answer>
                </task>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exercise-subspaces-symmetric-matrices">
        <title>The subspace of symmetric matrices</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the set <m>W</m> of symmetric matrices in the vector
                space <m>\matrixring_{n}(\R)=\matrixring_{n\times n}(\R)</m>.
                Show that for each <m>n</m>, <m>W</m> is a subspace of
                <m>\matrixring_{n}(\R)</m>.
                <!-->
                        Determine if the set
                        <md>
                            S=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2\\2 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}-1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}4 \amp 1\\1 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}\right\}
                        </md>
                        is a spanning set for <m>
			    W= 2\times2
			</m>
                        symmetric matrices.
                    <-->
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                <p>
                    We employ the Subspace Test:
                    <ol marker="(i)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Since the <m>n\times n</m> zero matrix
                                <m> \zerovec</m> satisfies
                                <m>\utrans{\zerovec}=\zerovec</m>, the zero
                                matrix is symmetric, so <m>W</m> is nonempty.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Let <m>A_{1}</m> and <m>A_{2}</m> be in
                                <m>W</m>.
                                Then <m>A_{1}</m> and <m>A_{2}</m> are both
                                symmetric <m>n\times n</m> matrices, so
                                <m>\utrans{A_1}=A_1</m> and
                                <m>\utrans{A_2}=A_2</m>.
                                We employ
                                <xref ref="proposition-matrix-ops-algebra-rules-transpose-add" text="custom"> Transpose Addition Rule </xref>
                                to demonstrate that <m>A_{1}+A_{2}</m> is also
                                symmetric:
                                <md>
                                    \utrans{(A_1+A_2)}=\utrans{A_1}+\utrans{A_2}=A_1+A_2
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Let <m>A</m> be in <m>W</m>, and let <m>k</m> be
                                a scalar.
                                Again, we know that <m>\utrans{A}=A</m>, so we
                                use the
                                <xref ref="proposition-matrix-ops-algebra-rules-transpose-smul" text="custom"> Transpose Scalar Multiplication Rule </xref>
                                to show that <m>kA</m> is also symmetric:
                                <md>
                                    \utrans{(kA)}=k\utrans{A}=kA
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <!--
                        <p>
                            If <m>A</m> is a symmetric matrix in
                            <m> \matrixring_{2}(\R)</m>, then <m>A</m> must be
                            of the form
                            <m>A=\begin{bmatrix}a \amp  b\\b \amp d\end{bmatrix}</m>
                            for some <m>a,b,d</m>.
                            We can then decompose <m>A</m> as
                            <md>
                                \begin{bmatrix}a \amp b\\b \amp d\end{bmatrix}=a\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}+b\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp1\\1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}+d\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}
                            </md>,
                            so
                            <m>T=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix},\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 1\\1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix},\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}\right\}</m>
                            is a spanning set for <m>W</m>.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            However, this is not the proposed spanning set
                            <m>
			    S=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2\\2 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}-1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}4 \amp 1\\1 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}\right\}
			</m>.
                            We can use
                            <xref ref="proposition-subspaces-eq-span-test" text="title"/>
                            to determine if this is spanning set for <m>W</m>.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Since the vectors in <m>S</m> are symmetric, every
                            vector in <m>S</m> is a linear combination of the
                            vectors in <m>T</m>, so now we need to check that
                            every vector in <m>T</m> is a linear combination of
                            the vectors in <m>S</m>.
                            Starting with
                            <m>\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                            we note that
                            <md>
                                \begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}=0\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2\\2 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}-1\begin{bmatrix}-1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}+0\begin{bmatrix}4 \amp 1\\1 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}.
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Now we tackle the less obvious linear combinations.
                            We take
                            <m>\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 1\\1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}</m>
                            and build an arbitrary linear combination using
                            <m> S</m>:
                            <md>
                                \begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 1\\1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}=k_1\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2\\2 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}+k_2\begin{bmatrix}-1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}+k_3\begin{bmatrix}4 \amp 1\\1 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}k_1-k_2+4k_3 \amp 2k_1+k_3\\2k_1+k_3 \amp k_1+k_3\end{bmatrix}
                            </md>
                            Comparing entries on each side of the equation leads
                            to the system of equations
                            <md>
                                \begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
			    k_1  \amp  - \amp   k_2 \amp  + \amp 4k_3 \amp = \amp 0 \text{,} \\
			2k_1 \amp \amp \amp + \amp   k_3       \amp = \amp 1 \text{,} \\
			2k_1 \amp \amp \amp + \amp   k_3       \amp = \amp 1\text{,} \\
			k_1 \amp \amp \amp + \amp   k_3       \amp = \amp 0
		\end{sysofeqns}
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Which we put into an augmented matrix and reduce:
                            <md>
                                \begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
			 1 \amp  -1 \amp  4   \amp 0\\
			2 \amp  0\amp  1    \amp 1 \\
			 2 \amp  0 \amp 1  \amp 1 \\
			 1 \amp 0\amp  1  \amp 0
		\end{abmatrix}
		\qquad\rowredarrow\qquad
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
			1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\\
			0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp   -3 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp  -1\\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
		\end{abmatrix}
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            The reduced matrix tells us that
                            <m>k_1=1, k_2=-3, k_3=-1</m>.
                            We can verify this:
                            <md>
                                1\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2\\2 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}-3\begin{bmatrix}-1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}-1\begin{bmatrix}4 \amp 1\\1 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1+3-4 \amp 2-1\\2-1 \amp 1-1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 1\\1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}
                            </md>.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            You can verify through a similar calculation that
                            <md>
                                -1\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2\\2 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}+7\begin{bmatrix}-1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}+2\begin{bmatrix}4 \amp 1\\1 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1+3-4 \amp 2-1\\2-1 \amp 1-1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}
                            </md>.
                            Since every vector in <m>T</m> is a also linear
                            combination of the vectors in <m>S</m>, this means
                            they span the same space, so <m>S</m> is a spanning
                            set for the <m>2\times 2</m> symmetric matrices.
                        </p>-->
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
</exercises>